Dubliners by James Joyce (1914), Project Gutenberg eBook 2814 (https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2814). Public domain in
Ireland and the USA. Its full text is in data/training/dubliners.txt, used by the lessons "The next word" and "AI detectors:
what a flag proves". What changed: the Project Gutenberg header and footer were removed.

Quick, Draw! dataset made available by Google, Inc.; drawings contributed by players. CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
https://github.com/googlecreativelab/quickdraw-dataset. The licence's disclaimer of warranties applies. Lesson 1.9
adapts it: data/interactive/doodle-guesser.json holds three of its drawings, shown as examples, and, kept for a test
that checks how they were chosen, the recognised test drawings that come before each one in its file;
data/doodle/six-kinds.weights.json and data/doodle/three-kinds.weights.json are numbers trained on 12,000 of them.
What changed: from the first 4 MiB of six of its simplified files (apple, car, cat, fish, house, umbrella) we took the
first 2,500 drawings of each kind that the game had recognised, kept only their strokes, drew each on a 28 by 28 grid,
trained two small models on the first 2,000 of each kind (the three-kinds model on apple, house and umbrella only) and
tested them on the other 500.
